-- statement:
--   This block formalizes pure type systems with fully annotated syntax over an arbitrary sort type S. Expressions are de Bruijn variables, sorts, applications, lambda abstractions with a domain annotation, and dependent products (pi) with a domain; renaming, simultaneous substitution (with lifting under binders) and single-variable instantiation are defined structurally. A Specification consists of an axiom relation between pairs of sorts and a ternary rule relation on sorts, with no functionality assumed for either. Beta is the one-step reduction generated by contracting (lam A b) a to b instantiated at a, closed under reduction in either side of an application and in the domain or body of lam and pi; Converts is the equivalence relation generated by Beta. An expression is Normal if it has no Beta step, WeaklyNormalizing if it reduces (reflexive-transitively) to a normal form, and StronglyNormalizing if it is accessible for the converse of Beta, so no infinite reduction exists, including reductions inside type annotations. Typing judgments HasType P Γ M A, with contexts as lists of types stored newest first, are inductively generated by: the axiom rule typing sort s by sort t in the empty context whenever the axioms relate s and t; variable 0 typed by the renamed type when the context is extended by a type of some sort; weakening; the product rule giving pi A B the sort s3 when A has sort s1, B has sort s2 in the extended context and the rule relates s1, s2, s3; abstraction, requiring the product type to be well-sorted; application, with result type the instantiation of the codomain at the argument; and conversion along Converts to a well-sorted type. ValidContext is generated from the empty context by adding a type that has some sort. An expression M is Legal in a context if for some A either M has type A or A has type M. SystemWeaklyNormalizing and SystemStronglyNormalizing are defined propositions saying that, for every valid context, every legal expression is weakly, respectively strongly, normalizing; no theorem about them is established in this block.
